    <section class="section" id="s1">
      <h2>1. What is Digital Forensics?</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>
        Digital forensics is the science of collecting, preserving, analyzing, and presenting digital evidence in a
        manner that is legally admissible. Think of it like traditional crime scene investigation — but instead of
        fingerprints and DNA, investigators work with <strong>RAM dumps, network packets, log files, and disk
          images</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The field emerged in the 1980s when law enforcement first encountered computers as tools of crime. By the early
        2000s, it had grown into a structured discipline with established methodologies, certified professionals, and
        dedicated software suites.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-info">
        <strong>🔍 Analogy</strong>
        Imagine a burglar breaks into a bank. Traditional forensics looks at physical clues — footprints, fingerprints.
        Digital forensics looks at the bank's CCTV footage (network logs), the alarm system's memory (RAM), and the
        vault access records (disk). Each layer tells a different part of the story.
      </div>

      <h3>The Five Branches of Digital Forensics</h3>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">💾</div>
          <h4>Disk Forensics</h4>
          <p>Examines hard drives, SSDs, USB devices for deleted files, partitions, metadata.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🧠</div>
          <h4>Memory Forensics</h4>
          <p>Analyzes volatile RAM to find running processes, injected code, encryption keys.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🌐</div>
          <h4>Network Forensics</h4>
          <p>Captures and analyzes network traffic to reconstruct attacks and data exfiltration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">📱</div>
          <h4>Mobile Forensics</h4>
          <p>Extracts evidence from smartphones — messages, GPS data, app usage.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">☁️</div>
          <h4>Cloud Forensics</h4>
          <p>Investigates incidents in cloud environments — AWS, Azure, GCP logs and artifacts.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

