      <h2>2. Memory Forensics — Deep Dive</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>
        Memory forensics (also called <strong>volatile memory analysis</strong> or <strong>RAM forensics</strong>)
        involves capturing and analyzing the contents of a computer's RAM at a specific point in time. Unlike a hard
        drive, RAM is <em>volatile</em> — it loses all data the moment the power is cut.
      </p>
      <p>
        This volatility is exactly what makes RAM forensics so valuable. Malware authors know that if they keep their
        malicious code only in memory (never writing to disk), traditional antivirus and disk forensics will miss them
        entirely. These are called <strong>"fileless malware"</strong> attacks.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <h3>Why RAM is a Gold Mine for Investigators</h3>
        <p>At any given moment, a running computer's RAM contains: active processes and their arguments, network socket
          connections, encryption keys (even for "secure" containers), user credentials, browser history (not yet
          written to disk), and code injected by malware into legitimate processes.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>What Lives in RAM?</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <ul class="styled">
            <li>Running processes and their threads</li>
            <li>Loaded DLLs and kernel modules</li>
            <li>Network connection tables</li>
            <li>Registry hive data (in-memory)</li>
            <li>Clipboard contents</li>
            <li>Decrypted data from encrypted volumes</li>
            <li>Bash/command history</li>
            <li>TLS session keys</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <ul class="styled">
            <li>Injected shellcode</li>
            <li>Rootkit hooks</li>
            <li>Password hashes</li>
            <li>Browser cached pages</li>
            <li>Email contents being composed</li>
            <li>Keylogger buffers</li>
            <li>Screen captures in memory</li>
            <li>IRC/C2 chat logs</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Memory Acquisition Methods</h3>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Hardware-Based Acquisition</h4>
            <p>Uses a hardware device (like a FireWire interface or PCIe card) to directly read physical RAM. Most
              forensically sound but requires physical access.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Software-Based Acquisition</h4>
            <p>Tools like <strong>WinPmem</strong>, <strong>LiME</strong> (Linux), <strong>osxpmem</strong> (macOS), or
              <strong>DumpIt</strong> create a raw memory image file (.raw, .dmp, .lime). Quick and practical.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Virtual Machine Snapshots</h4>
            <p>Hypervisors (VMware, VirtualBox, Hyper-V) can create VM snapshots that include full memory state. Files
              like <code>.vmem</code> or <code>.vmss</code> can be directly analyzed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Crash Dumps</h4>
            <p>Windows creates crash dump files (<code>MEMORY.DMP</code>) during BSODs. These can be analyzed
              forensically even after the fact.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

