    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
    <section class="section" id="s4">
      <h2>4. What is PCAP?</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>
        <strong>PCAP</strong> (Packet CAPture) is both a file format and an API for capturing network traffic at the
        packet level. It was developed by Van Jacobson, Craig Leres, and Steven McCanne at Lawrence Berkeley National
        Laboratory in the late 1980s as part of the <code>tcpdump</code> project.
      </p>
      <p>
        A PCAP file is like a <strong>DVR recording of your network</strong> — every packet that crosses a network
        interface is saved with its exact bytes and a precise timestamp. Investigators can replay, filter, decode, and
        reconstruct sessions from this recording days, weeks, or even years later.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-info">
        <strong>💡 Key Fact</strong>
        The libpcap library (Linux/macOS) and WinPcap/Npcap (Windows) are the underlying capture engines used by nearly
        every network tool — Wireshark, tcpdump, Snort, Zeek, and hundreds more all capture via this same standardized
        API.
      </div>

      <h3>PCAP vs. PCAPng</h3>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>PCAP (Classic)</th>
            <th>PCAPng (Next Generation)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Extension</td>
            <td>.pcap</td>
            <td>.pcapng</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Timestamps</td>
            <td>Microsecond precision</td>
            <td>Nanosecond precision</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Multiple interfaces</td>
            <td>❌ Single interface</td>
            <td>✅ Multiple interfaces</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Comments</td>
            <td>❌ Not supported</td>
            <td>✅ Per-packet comments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Compression</td>
            <td>❌ No native support</td>
            <td>✅ Supported</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tool support</td>
            <td>Universal</td>
            <td>Modern tools only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Block structure</td>
            <td>Simple linear</td>
            <td>Extensible block-based</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>How is PCAP Captured?</h3>
      <p>Capture happens by putting a network interface into <strong>promiscuous mode</strong> — instead of only
        processing packets addressed to that machine, the NIC accepts all frames on the segment. The OS then forwards
        these to the capture library.</p>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <strong>⚠️ Legal Warning</strong>
        Capturing network traffic without authorization is illegal in most jurisdictions under laws like the Computer
        Fraud and Abuse Act (USA), Computer Misuse Act (UK), and similar statutes globally. Always obtain written
        authorization before capturing traffic.
      </div>
    </section>

