      <h2>6. Tools for PCAP Analysis</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>The PCAP analysis ecosystem has matured into a rich set of tools — from beginner-friendly GUIs to scriptable
        command-line powerhouses.</p>

      <h3>Wireshark — The King of GUI Analysis</h3>
      <p>
        Wireshark is the world's most widely used network protocol analyzer. It provides a graphical interface for live
        capture and offline analysis of thousands of protocols. Key features include color-coded packet display,
        powerful display filters, stream following (reconstruct TCP/HTTP sessions), and protocol dissectors.
      </p>
      <div class="tags">
        <span class="tag">display filter</span><span class="tag">tcp.stream</span>
        <span class="tag">follow tcp stream</span><span class="tag">export objects</span>
        <span class="tag">IO graphs</span><span class="tag">tshark CLI</span>
      </div>

      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><span class="comment"># Wireshark display filter examples</span>
<span class="keyword">ip.addr</span> == 192.168.1.105            <span class="comment"># Filter by IP address</span>
<span class="keyword">tcp.port</span> == 4444                    <span class="comment"># Common Metasploit reverse shell port</span>
<span class="keyword">http.request.method</span> == "POST"       <span class="comment"># Find POST requests (data exfil?)</span>
<span class="keyword">dns.qry.name contains</span> ".onion"      <span class="comment"># Tor hidden service lookups</span>
<span class="keyword">tcp.flags.syn</span> == 1 &amp;&amp; <span class="keyword">tcp.flags.ack</span> == 0  <span class="comment"># SYN packets (port scan)</span>
<span class="keyword">frame contains</span> "password"           <span class="comment"># Search payload for keyword</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3>tcpdump — The Command-Line Workhorse</h3>
      <p>tcpdump is the classic Unix packet capture utility, ideal for capturing on headless servers and automating
        forensic workflows via shell scripts.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><span class="comment"># Capture 1000 packets on eth0 to file</span>
tcpdump -i eth0 -c 1000 -w capture.pcap

<span class="comment"># Capture only TCP port 80 traffic</span>
tcpdump -i any 'tcp port 80' -w http.pcap

<span class="comment"># Read and display with verbose output</span>
tcpdump -r capture.pcap -v -X

<span class="comment"># Capture traffic between two specific hosts</span>
tcpdump -i eth0 'host 10.0.0.5 and host 10.0.0.10'

<span class="comment"># Filter for large packets (possible data exfil)</span>
tcpdump -i eth0 'greater 1400'</pre>
      </div>

