    <!-- SECTION 7 -->
    <section class="section" id="s7">
      <h2>7. Step-by-Step PCAP Investigation</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>When presented with a PCAP file during an incident response or CTF challenge, a systematic approach ensures
        nothing is missed. Here is a proven methodology.</p>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>File Validation &amp; Initial Triage</h4>
            <p>Verify the PCAP is not corrupt using <code>capinfos capture.pcap</code>. Note: total packets, capture
              duration, average packet rate, link type. A capture showing 50,000 packets in 2 seconds might indicate a
              DoS attack or port scan.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre>capinfos suspicious.pcap
<span class="comment"># Output: File size, packets, duration, data rate, etc.</span></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Protocol &amp; Endpoint Statistics</h4>
            <p>In Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → Protocol Hierarchy</strong> shows protocol breakdown.
              <strong>Statistics → Endpoints</strong> reveals all communicating IP/MAC addresses. <strong>Statistics →
                Conversations</strong> shows who talked to whom and how much data was exchanged.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Identify Suspicious IPs &amp; Ports</h4>
            <p>Look for connections to unusual ports (non-standard HTTP, high-numbered ports), external IPs not in the
              expected range, or internal hosts communicating with TOR exit nodes or known C2 infrastructure.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><span class="comment"># Find all unique destination IPs</span>
tshark -r capture.pcap -T fields -e ip.dst | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Follow TCP/HTTP Streams</h4>
            <p>Right-click any TCP packet → Follow → TCP Stream. This reconstructs the full application-layer
              conversation as text. For HTTP, you can see complete requests and responses, revealing C2 commands, data
              exfiltration, or web exploits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Extract Files and Artifacts</h4>
            <p>Wireshark: <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP</strong> extracts all transferred files. NetworkMiner
              automatically extracts images, PDFs, executables. Tshark can dump raw streams for manual analysis.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><span class="comment"># Extract all HTTP objects</span>
tshark -r capture.pcap --export-objects http,./extracted_files/</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">6</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>DNS Analysis</h4>
            <p>DNS is a favorite channel for C2 and data exfiltration. Look for: unusually long subdomains (DNS
              tunneling), high-frequency queries to a single domain, queries for non-existent domains (DGA malware), and
              responses with very long TXT records.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <pre><span class="comment"># Extract all DNS queries</span>
tshark -r capture.pcap -Y "dns" -T fields -e dns.qry.name | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">7</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Timeline Reconstruction</h4>
            <p>Build a timeline of events using packet timestamps. Correlate spikes in traffic with specific protocols
              or connections. Tools like NetworkMiner and custom Python scripts using <code>pyshark</code> or
              <code>scapy</code> can automate timeline generation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 8 -->
    <section class="section" id="s8">
      <h2>8. Common Attack Patterns in PCAP</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>Knowing what attacks look like at the packet level is the core skill of a network forensics investigator.</p>

      <h3>Port Scanning (Nmap SYN Scan)</h3>
      <p>A SYN scan sends TCP SYN packets to hundreds of ports rapidly without completing the three-way handshake. In
        PCAP it looks like many packets from one source to one destination, targeting sequential or common ports, all
        with SYN flag only, most resulting in RST (closed) or no response (filtered).</p>

      <div class="diagram-wrap">
