            <td>Statistical analysis of inter-packet timing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Lateral Movement</td>
            <td>SMB connections between internal hosts, PSEXEC patterns</td>
            <td><code>smb2 &amp;&amp; ip.src == [internal]</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 9 -->
    <section class="section" id="s9">
      <h2>9. Memory Forensics with Volatility</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p>
        <strong>Volatility</strong> is the most widely used open-source memory forensics framework, supporting analysis
        of Windows, Linux, and macOS memory dumps. It works by reconstructing operating system data structures from the
        raw memory dump bytes.
      </p>

      <h3>Essential Volatility 3 Commands</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><span class="comment"># List all running processes</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.pslist

<span class="comment"># Show process tree (parent-child relationships)</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.pstree

<span class="comment"># Find processes with suspicious parent-child relationships</span>
<span class="comment"># e.g., Word.exe spawning cmd.exe (macro execution)</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.pstree | grep -A2 WINWORD

<span class="comment"># List active network connections</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.netstat

<span class="comment"># Scan for injected code (process injection detection)</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.malfind

<span class="comment"># Dump a suspicious process to file for further analysis</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.dumpfiles --pid 1337

<span class="comment"># Extract command line arguments of all processes</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.cmdline

<span class="comment"># Show loaded DLLs for a specific process</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.dlllist --pid 1337

<span class="comment"># Registry hive analysis</span>
python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.registry.hivelist</pre>
      </div>

      <h3>Process Injection Detection with malfind</h3>
      <p>
        The <code>malfind</code> plugin is one of Volatility's most powerful features. It scans all process memory
        regions looking for pages that are marked as executable but were not loaded from a file on disk — a hallmark of
        <strong>process injection</strong> attacks.
      </p>
      <div class="callout callout-danger">
        <strong>🚨 Malware Indicator</strong>
        If malfind reports a memory region starting with <code>MZ</code> (Windows PE header magic bytes) inside a
        process like <code>explorer.exe</code> or <code>svchost.exe</code>, an attacker has likely injected a malicious
        executable directly into that process's memory — a technique called "process hollowing" or "reflective DLL
        injection."
      </div>

      <h3>Common Volatility Forensic Findings</h3>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🦠</div>
          <h4>Fileless Malware</h4>
          <p>No disk artifact. Malware lives entirely in memory as injected shellcode or a hollowed process.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🔑</div>
          <h4>Credential Dumping</h4>
          <p>Password hashes, plaintext credentials, and Kerberos tickets extracted from LSASS process memory.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🕵️</div>
          <h4>Rootkit Detection</h4>
          <p>SSDT hooks, DKOM (Direct Kernel Object Manipulation), and hidden processes revealed by cross-view analysis.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🌐</div>
          <h4>C2 Connections</h4>
          <p>Active and recently closed connections to attacker infrastructure visible in netstat output from memory.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

