    <!-- SECTION 11 -->
    <section class="section" id="s11">
      <h2>11. Real-World Case Studies</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>

      <h3>Case Study 1: Emotet Banking Trojan Investigation</h3>
      <p>
        A financial institution detected anomalous outbound traffic from a workstation. Initial PCAP analysis showed
        periodic HTTP POST requests to a random-looking domain (DGA-generated) with Base64-encoded bodies. Memory
        forensics revealed <code>winword.exe</code> had spawned <code>powershell.exe</code> with an encoded command,
        which then injected code into <code>explorer.exe</code>. The PCAP payload, when decoded, contained the Emotet
        loader requesting its full payload. Combined analysis confirmed the initial infection vector (malicious Word
        macro), the persistence mechanism (registry run key found in memory), and the C2 server.
      </p>
      <div class="callout callout-success">
        <strong>✅ Lesson Learned</strong>
        Correlating the parent process chain in memory (Word → PowerShell → Explorer) with the network beacon timing
        allowed investigators to pinpoint the exact email attachment that caused the infection, enabling targeted email
        hygiene improvements.
      </div>

      <h3>Case Study 2: Ransomware Pre-Encryption Stage</h3>
      <p>
        Network monitoring captured a PCAP showing SMB (port 445) lateral movement across 23 internal hosts within 4
        minutes. Memory forensics of the initial infected host showed <code>PsExec</code> artifacts and an injected
        payload in <code>svchost.exe</code> that contained a hardcoded AES key. This key was extracted from memory —
        crucially, <em>before</em> encryption began on most hosts. The key was used to proactively decrypt files that
        had already been encrypted, saving the organization from paying a ransom.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 3: DNS Exfiltration in Healthcare</h3>
      <p>
        A healthcare provider noticed an employee workstation making thousands of DNS queries per hour to a subdomain.
        PCAP analysis revealed subdomain labels that were 60–80 characters long — far beyond normal DNS usage. Decoded,
        they contained patient records (encrypted with a simple XOR). Memory forensics identified a custom Python
        interpreter running hidden from the taskbar, with the exfiltration script loaded entirely in memory. No file was
        ever written to disk, making disk forensics useless — only the RAM dump preserved the evidence.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 4: APT Campaign — Cobalt Strike Beacon</h3>
      <p>
        A government agency's SOC detected suspicious HTTPS traffic to a CDN-hosted domain. PCAP showed encrypted data
        but with unusual JA3 TLS fingerprints (Cobalt Strike's default fingerprint). Memory forensics of the beaconing
        host found a Cobalt Strike reflective loader injected into <code>notepad.exe</code> — classic
        living-off-the-land technique. The memory dump preserved the full Cobalt Strike configuration including the C2
        staging URI, sleep interval, and public key — crucial intelligence for threat attribution.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 12 -->
    <section class="section" id="s12">
      <h2>12. Advantages, Limitations &amp; Best Practices</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3 style="color:#2ecc71;">✅ Advantages</h3>
          <ul class="styled">
            <li>PCAP provides irrefutable evidence of network activity — exact bytes, timestamps, source/destination
            </li>
            <li>Memory forensics captures evidence that no disk-based tool can find (fileless malware)</li>
            <li>Both methods are non-destructive when done correctly</li>
            <li>Combined analysis provides the most complete attack timeline</li>
            <li>Evidence can be replayed and re-analyzed as new threat intelligence emerges</li>
            <li>Supports legal proceedings with provable chain of custody</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="color:#e74c3c;">❌ Limitations</h3>
          <ul class="styled">
            <li>PCAP storage is expensive — 1 Gbps network generates ~450 GB/hour</li>
            <li>TLS 1.3 encryption makes PCAP payloads unreadable without keys</li>
            <li>RAM is volatile — evidence lost on reboot if not acquired in time</li>
            <li>Sophisticated attackers can detect forensic tools running in memory</li>
            <li>Requires specialized expertise to interpret correctly</li>
            <li>Legal challenges around PCAP collection (wiretapping laws)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Best Practices</h3>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Always Document Chain of Custody</h4>
            <p>Hash all captured files immediately (SHA-256). Document who collected what, when, and how. Any break in
              chain of custody can render evidence inadmissible in court.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Capture RAM Before Disk</h4>
            <p>Following the "Order of Volatility" — RAM first (most volatile), then swap/page file, then disk. This
              ensures the most time-sensitive evidence is captured first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Use Write Blockers for Disk Evidence</h4>
            <p>For any disk evidence, always use a write blocker to prevent accidental modification of the original
              evidence.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Correlate Multiple Evidence Sources</h4>
            <p>PCAP + RAM dump + OS logs + endpoint telemetry. The more sources corroborate each other, the stronger the
              evidence.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h4>Know Your Legal Authority</h4>
            <p>Always obtain written authorization before capturing traffic. In corporate environments, ensure
              acceptable use policies cover forensic monitoring. In legal cases, obtain proper warrants.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

